<% content_for :title, "Perspectives on Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-perspectives-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-hero">
    <div class="addiction-perspectives-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-perspectives-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / PERSPECTIVES
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>ADDICTION.</span>
        <span>ANOTHER WAY</span>
        <span>TO SEE IT.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-perspectives-intro">
        MORE THAN ONE STORY.<br>
        MORE THAN ONE FUCKING LENS.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 / THE BIG IDEA -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-pink">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">01 / THE BIG IDEA</p>

      <h2>
        MORE THAN<br>
        ONE WAY<br>
        IN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <blockquote>
        “If my life wasn’t funny it would just be true,
        and that is unacceptable.”
        <br><br>
        — Carrie Fisher
      </blockquote>

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Addiction can be understood through behavior, suffering,
        biology, relationships, spirituality, and the environments
        in which you learn to live.
      </p>

      <p>
        Each perspective brings something into focus. What you notice
        through one may be harder to see through another. Looking
        through several gives you more ways to understand your history
        and participate in what comes next.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can be comfortable in your sobriety and still examine
        addiction. Reflection does not have to begin with a crisis.
        Sometimes having some stability finally gives you room to ask
        better questions.
      </p>

      <p>
        What did drinking do for you? What did it cost? What became
        familiar enough that you stopped questioning it? What kind
        of life are you building now that alcohol no longer organizes
        so much of it?
      </p>

      <p>
        Those questions also make room for mental illness. Bipolar
        disorder and addiction can affect the same life without
        explaining every part of each other. Your moods, habits,
        circumstances, and decisions deserve attention in their own
        right. Understanding their relationship takes more precision
        than attributing every difficult experience to a diagnosis.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 / SUFFERING AND COMPASSION -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-yellow">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">02 / SUFFERING & COMPASSION</p>

      <h2>
        LOOK<br>
        A LITTLE<br>
        CLOSER.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Buddhist perspectives begin by taking suffering seriously.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <em>Dhammapada</em>, <em>What the Buddha Taught</em>,
        and <em>The Heart of the Buddha’s Teaching</em> invite you
        to examine experience: how you meet discomfort, what you
        cling to, what you resist, and what happens when you expect
        changing circumstances to provide lasting satisfaction.
        <em>Recovery Dharma</em> brings Buddhist practices directly
        into the work of recovery.
      </p>

      <p>
        Through that lens, addiction involves the relationship you
        develop with relief. Something changes how you feel, and
        you begin returning to it. Over time, the immediate effect
        can become more compelling than the larger experience.
        You remember what alcohol offered while giving less
        attention to what followed.
      </p>

      <p>
        That pattern deserves curiosity. What felt difficult to
        inhabit? Pain, uncertainty, loneliness, excitement, or
        simply an ordinary evening? What expectations did you
        bring to those experiences? Did discomfort begin to feel
        unacceptable unless you could change it immediately?
      </p>

      <p>
        You can investigate those questions without judging yourself
        for having wanted relief. Pain hurts. Depression can be
        exhausting. Uncertainty can occupy your mind for hours.
        Understanding suffering gives you information about the
        care and support your life requires.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mindfulness helps you notice what is happening with greater
        accuracy. Compassion helps you remain present long enough
        to understand it. Together, they can make room for a response
        that considers more than the immediate moment.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 / UNDERSTANDING YOUR PARTS -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-purple">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">03 / UNDERSTANDING YOUR PARTS</p>

      <h2>
        WHAT WAS<br>
        IT DOING<br>
        FOR YOU?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Understanding an intention does not require approving
        the action.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>No Bad Parts</em> offers a way to approach inner conflict
        through parts of you that carry different needs, fears,
        and protective intentions. Applied to addiction, that means
        asking what a behavior was trying to accomplish, even when
        its consequences were harmful.
      </p>

      <p>
        Perhaps drinking once offered social confidence, distance
        from pain, or permission to stop managing everything for
        a while. A part of you may have learned that alcohol worked
        quickly enough to become familiar. Another part may have
        watched the consequences with anger, fear, or shame.
        You can listen to both.
      </p>

      <p>
        Understanding an intention helps you identify what still
        needs attention. If you wanted connection, where can
        connection develop now? If you wanted freedom, what forms
        of freedom are actually available to you? If you wanted
        rest, how much space does your life allow for it?
      </p>

      <p>
        This perspective also changes how you approach yourself.
        Instead of treating your mind as an enemy you must defeat,
        you can become interested in the conflicts within it.
        Your task is to make decisions that consider your whole
        life, including the needs that an immediate solution overlooks.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 / THOUGHTS AND LANGUAGE -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-black">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">04 / THOUGHTS & LANGUAGE</p>

      <h2>
        QUESTION<br>
        THE FUCKING<br>
        STORY.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Give yourself an account of reality that is specific
        enough to help you act.
      </p>

      <p>
        David Burns’s <em>Feeling Good</em> offers tools for
        identifying distorted thinking. Addiction gives those
        tools plenty to work with. So do shame, depression,
        anxiety, and the stories you build around your own behavior.
      </p>

      <p>
        You might turn a mistake into a permanent identity.
        You might assume you know what everyone thinks of you.
        You might dismiss progress because it falls short of
        the standard you imagined. You can also think unrealistically
        in a positive direction, minimizing consequences or assuming
        that confidence guarantees good judgment.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>The Four Agreements</em> brings another set of questions
        about language, assumptions, personal interpretations,
        and effort. Consider how your words shape your experience.
      </p>

      <div class="ap-thoughts">
        <p>“I HAVE CONSEQUENCES TO ADDRESS.”</p>
        <p>“I RUIN EVERYTHING.”</p>
      </div>

      <p>
        The first leaves you with a task. The second gives you
        a sweeping verdict with very little practical direction.
        You do not have to talk yourself into feeling wonderful.
        Give yourself an account of reality that is specific
        enough to help you act.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 / HABITS AND STRUCTURE -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-pink">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">05 / HABITS & STRUCTURE</p>

      <h2>
        BUILD<br>
        A LIFE<br>
        THAT FITS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Recovery takes place in ordinary days.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Atomic Habits</em> directs attention toward the routines,
        cues, and environments that make repeated behavior easier.
        You have mornings, meals, appointments, money,
        responsibilities, and hours that need filling. The structure
        of those days affects what becomes familiar.
      </p>

      <p>
        Small actions repeated consistently can begin to change
        what your life supports. Build around your actual
        circumstances. If pain limits your mobility, your routines
        need to accommodate that. If your energy and concentration
        fluctuate, leave room for variation.
      </p>

      <p>
        If you tend to avoid a task when it becomes overwhelming,
        make the next action concrete enough to begin.
      </p>

      <p>
        Habits can support stability without guaranteeing it.
        Mental illness can still interrupt a carefully arranged
        life. A useful system includes ways to adjust, communicate,
        and accept help when your capacity changes.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 / ACCOUNTABILITY -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-yellow">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">06 / ACCOUNTABILITY</p>

      <h2>
        OWN<br>
        YOUR<br>
        SHIT.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Being an adult means attending to what your behavior
        leaves behind.
      </p>

      <p>
        AA and NA bring recovery into shared experience and action.
        Their approaches ask you to examine your behavior honestly,
        accept help, consider the effects on other people,
        and practice living differently.
      </p>

      <p>
        That can be uncomfortable. You may understand the context
        of a harmful decision very well. You may have been ill,
        frightened, impaired, or overwhelmed. Those circumstances
        belong in an accurate account. Other people’s experiences
        belong there too.
      </p>

      <p>
        Address the bill. Have the conversation. Make the apology.
        Respect a boundary. Follow through on a commitment,
        or communicate when you cannot.
      </p>

      <p>
        Brené Brown’s <em>The Gifts of Imperfection</em> helps make
        room for doing that work without requiring perfection
        as the price of worthiness. You can acknowledge something
        serious while remaining willing to care for yourself.
      </p>

      <p>
        Guilt can direct your attention toward an action that needs
        repair. Shame can turn your entire identity into the problem.
        Keep asking what the feeling helps you do. Accountability
        needs enough specificity to guide change.
      </p>

      <p>
        You also do not owe an apology for having an illness
        or needing support. Responsibility includes recognizing
        your limits and preparing for them honestly.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 / COMMUNITY AND YOUR OWN PATH -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-purple">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">07 / COMMUNITY & YOUR OWN PATH</p>

      <h2>
        FIND<br>
        YOUR WAY.<br>
        LET PEOPLE IN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        Independence can include accepting that your life
        is connected to other lives.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mary Beth O’Connor’s <em>From Junkie to Judge</em> adds
        a secular, individualized recovery account to the
        conversation. Her story gives you room to consider how
        you build a recovery that fits your beliefs and circumstances.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can examine a program carefully, disagree with parts
        of it, and still recognize something useful. You can also
        decide that another approach serves you better. The important
        question is how you participate in the approach you choose.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>The Red Road to Wellbriety</em> brings a culturally
        grounded Native American perspective on healing, community,
        and connection. Its cultural context deserves respect.
        It also broadens the conversation beyond individual behavior
        by drawing attention to belonging and the relationships
        through which healing takes place.
      </p>

      <p>
        Consider where your own recovery has somewhere to live.
        Who knows you honestly? Where can you contribute?
        What relationships support your well-being? What gives
        you a sense of continuity and purpose?
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 / JUDGMENT AND ACTION -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-black">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">08 / JUDGMENT & ACTION</p>

      <h2>
        WORK WITH<br>
        WHAT<br>
        YOU HAVE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        You can acknowledge that something is difficult
        and still ask what wisdom requires of you now.
      </p>

      <p>
        Stoicism and <em>The Stoic Challenge</em> invite you to
        examine your judgments and respond resourcefully to
        circumstances. You cannot revise the past or guarantee
        the future. You can look carefully at the situation
        in front of you and consider what response is possible.
      </p>

      <p>
        That might involve patience, a practical adjustment,
        an honest admission, or accepting help. Sometimes it means
        recognizing that anger about a circumstance is consuming
        the energy you need to address it. You do not have to
        describe adversity as a gift.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <em>Bhagavad Gita</em> adds the discipline of acting
        without demanding control over the fruits of action.
        You can do something worthwhile without receiving
        the outcome you wanted on your preferred schedule.
      </p>

      <p>
        Treatment, sobriety, honesty, and effort matter even when
        life remains uncertain. Their value extends beyond whether
        they make today feel easier.
      </p>

      <p>
        The <em>Upanishads</em> invite reflection on the Self
        and your relationship to the changing experiences of body
        and mind. In the <em>Katha Upanishad’s</em> chariot image,
        the relationships among the rider, charioteer, reins,
        and horses offer a way to contemplate direction and discernment.
      </p>

      <p>
        For your own reflection, ask what is guiding your life.
        Immediate feeling? Familiar habit? Fear? Considered values?
        What helps you find your direction again when those
        influences compete?
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 / COURAGE -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-pink">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">09 / COURAGE</p>

      <h2>
        STRENGTH<br>
        CAN BE<br>
        QUIET.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        You can have dignity while needing treatment.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Shambhala: The Sacred Path of the Warrior</em> approaches
        courage through basic goodness, openness, and a willingness
        to encounter life. That gives you a different way to think
        about strength.
      </p>

      <p>
        You can be strong while admitting uncertainty. You can face
        something painful without attacking yourself for finding
        it painful.
      </p>

      <p>
        Living with addiction or mental illness can involve long
        periods of ordinary effort. Keeping an appointment.
        Telling the truth. Returning to a routine. Accepting
        a limitation without deciding that your future has disappeared.
      </p>

      <p>
        Those actions may attract very little attention.
        They still shape a life.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 10 / IDENTITY AND CREATIVITY -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-section ap-yellow">

    <div class="ap-heading">
      <p class="ap-number">10 / IDENTITY & CREATIVITY</p>

      <h2>
        STILL<br>
        FUCKING<br>
        INTERESTING.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="ap-copy">

      <p class="ap-lead">
        You can be sober and still have an interesting mind.
        Put it to work.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Kitchen Confidential</em> draws you into a world where
        craft, pressure, belonging, and excess coexist.
        <em>High Achiever</em> explores addiction, secrecy,
        consequences, and recovery with humor.
      </p>

      <p>
        Together, they invite you to consider how behavior becomes
        embedded in an environment—and how much can remain hidden
        behind someone’s outward role.
      </p>

      <p>
        You may also recognize the appeal of being the entertaining
        one, the fearless one, or the person with the wildest story.
        Giving up destructive behavior can raise a surprisingly
        personal question: who are you going to be now?
      </p>

      <p>
        You get to explore that. Humor, confidence, creativity,
        and individuality deserve room in your life. Give them
        opportunities to develop through things you want to keep doing.
      </p>

      <p>
        Carrie Fisher’s <em>Wishful Drinking</em> brings wit and
        candor to addiction and bipolar disorder. Her voice makes
        room for absurdity alongside suffering. You can talk
        seriously about your life and still be funny as hell.
      </p>

      <p>
        Gucci Mane’s autobiography makes sobriety part of a larger
        account of change involving creativity, identity, and his
        future. Zappa’s place in this discussion is different:
        his outspoken opposition to recreational drugs and his
        unconventional creative life offer another angle on the
        assumption that intoxication is necessary for originality.
      </p>

      <p>
        <em>Greenlights</em> offers another approach to looking back:
        finding meaning and direction in experiences that initially
        appeared to obstruct you. You can consider that perspective
        without forcing every loss into a lesson.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some things remain painful. You can still make decisions
        about the life that follows them.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-perspectives-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="ap-ending-small">
        A PAST TO UNDERSTAND. A PRESENT TO INHABIT.
      </p>

      <h2>
        CHANGE<br>
        THE<br>
        <span>LENS.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="ap-ending-copy">

      <p>
        You do not need to make every day a lesson about addiction.
        You can enjoy stability. You can be proud of sobriety.
        You can spend an afternoon absorbed in something that has
        absolutely fuck-all to do with either diagnosis.
      </p>

      <p>
        The perspectives remain available when you want them.
        Buddhism can help you examine suffering. Cognitive approaches
        can help you question a conclusion. IFS can help you understand
        an inner conflict. Stoicism can help you consider your
        response. Recovery communities can give honesty somewhere
        to be heard.
      </p>

      <p>
        Let those ideas inform your life. Test them through experience.
        Keep asking whether they help you become more aware,
        responsible, compassionate, and able to participate
        in what matters to you.
      </p>

      <p>
        You have a past to understand and a present worth inhabiting.
      </p>

      <p>
        Give both the attention they deserve.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "ap-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>